program            = 003_wide_and_unicode_literals
area               = 11_literals_and_strings
description        = Wide, UTF-8, 16-bit and 32-bit string and character literals, indexed in place with every element cast explicitly
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction:
- No string literal is ever modified. Every literal is read only, through indexing.
- No signed integer overflow. Every code point is at most 0x7FFF and every element is
  widened to long long or unsigned long long, both 64-bit on all four targets, before
  being printed. Loop counters are int and bounded by a literal element count.
- No shift operations of any kind, so no shift count can be out of range.
- No out-of-bounds access. Each loop bound is the literal's own element count,
  computed as sizeof literal divided by sizeof of its first element, so the loop
  visits exactly the elements including the terminating null and never one past it.
  The explicit terminator reads index count-1, which is the last valid element.
- No aliasing violation. The UTF-8 literal's elements are read and converted through
  unsigned char, which is always permitted; the other literals are read through their
  own element type by indexing the literal directly.
- No uninitialized read. Every value read is an element of a string literal, and zero
  has a static initializer.
- Every universal-character-name obeys C11 6.4.3p2: no UCN specifies a short
  identifier below 00A0 other than 0024. Plain ASCII characters are written directly
  rather than as UCNs, because a UCN for them would be ill-formed.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points; no argument to any call has a
  side effect; nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of unrelated
  objects; no address or pointer value is printed.
- Verified empirically: the default strict warning gate (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic
  -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror) produces zero diagnostics, and
  -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all runs to completion with no
  output and exit status 0. -pedantic is retained deliberately: the four literal
  prefixes are standard C11, not GNU extensions.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
The three C11 character type names are never written anywhere in the program, not
even in a comment, and the hazards they represent are handled by construction rather
than by a divergence marker:

1. The wide character type is signed int on x86_64, i686 and riscv64 but unsigned int
   on aarch64 under AAPCS64. Naming it and relying on its signedness would produce a
   spurious cross-backend divergence. Instead every wide element is indexed in place
   and cast explicitly to long long before printing.
2. The 16-bit and 32-bit character type names cannot be named at all: they are
   uchar.h typedefs, and uchar.h and wchar.h are not among bcc's nine bundled
   freestanding headers (stddef.h, stdint.h, stdarg.h, stdbool.h, limits.h, float.h,
   stdalign.h, stdnoreturn.h, iso646.h), while no corpus program may include any
   header. Every 16-bit and 32-bit element is therefore indexed in place and cast
   explicitly to unsigned long long.
3. All code point values are restricted to the range 0x00 to 0x7FFF, so no printed
   value can be affected by the signedness difference in point 1: every value is
   positive in every candidate element type.
4. The UTF-8 literal's element type is plain char, whose signedness is itself
   implementation-defined (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and
   riscv64), so each UTF-8 code unit is converted through unsigned char before being
   widened.
5. Array extents are printed as element counts, computed as sizeof literal divided by
   sizeof of its first element, never as byte counts, so no element-width assumption
   reaches the output. long long and unsigned long long are 64-bit on all four
   targets, and no long, size_t or pointer is printed, so the i686 width difference
   (sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 elsewhere) has no effect.

Documentation note, recorded for reference only and deliberately NOT minted as an
expected divergence: support for the u8, u, U and L literal prefixes is not
enumerated in the documented literal inventory. docs/technical-specifications.md line
99 and line 498 list numeric and string or character literal parsing with escape
sequences and the u, l, ll and f suffixes, but none of the four prefixes; line 497's
44-keyword C11 list contains neither the 16-bit nor the 32-bit character type name;
and recursive searches of docs/ for unicode, char16, char32 and wchar return no
matches. No marker is attached, because a marker on a program that does not diverge
yields an unexpected success, which fails the run. Should a divergence ever be
observed and traced to this gap, it must be minted both here and in
tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md, whose consistency is checked in both
directions. Until then any divergence here is correctly classified as a finding.

The source file is pure US-ASCII: every non-ASCII character appears only as a
universal-character-name escape.
END
